    "body": "Great on any Friday: OTTOCAST 2026 Play2Video - Wireless CarPlay & Android Auto Video Adapter\nBMW Australia Replacing iX Electric SUV With New iX5\n18 photos\nPhoto: BMW\nThe iX for Australia isn't going the way of the dodo because it sold worse than its direct rivals. BMW is pushing it out in favor of the iX5, a model donning new-gen styling and a more familiar badge. And while it may feature CLAR underpinnings, the iX5 levels up to cylindrical cells, 800 volts for ultra-fast charging, plus cell-to-pack construction, putting it closer to the Neue Klasse-based iX3 than the outgoing iX.\nGiven the aforementioned, keeping the iX and the incoming iX5 under the same roof makes little sense for the Australian subsidiary. Both are large zero-emission luxury SUVs, and there simply isn't enough daylight between them to justify selling both concomitantly. The iX, therefore, will be phased out locally. BMW hasn't confirmed when iX production wraps up, though.\nBrendan Michel, the head of product at BMW Australia, confirmed its canning to CarExpert. Hearsay, meanwhile, suggests that iX production will be wrapped up at some point in June 2028. The iX has already been discontinued from the United States and Canadian markets. Production for these markets ended in October 2026.\nBoth decisions speak volumes about how the Munich-based automaker's electric vehicle strategy is changing. Compared to the iX, which rolled out in late 2020 as a standalone statement with its own styling and a unique platform that happens to be highly compatible with the Cluster Architecture, the iX5 doesn't need an entirely separate model identity from the already familiar X5.\nFrom a commercial standpoint, BMW Australia has a compelling reason to make that switch. CarExpert highlights that only 2,424 units were shipped to Australia since its 2021 launch there, averaging in the ballpark of 500 units per annum. X5 deliveries over the same period reached just around 17,000 vehicles with gasoline, diesel, and plug-in hybrid powertrains.\nPhoto: BMW The redesigned X5 is coming with five confirmed powertrain flavors. The all-electric and hydrogen options bring the grand total to five, although the fuel-cell powertrain will be available in very few units and in select markets. Officially dubbed iX5 Hydrogen, that fellow is due in 2028 with confirmed exclusions that include Australia and Eastern Europe.\nMore than adequately powerful in 60 xDrive form, the full-electric iX5 for Australia is apparently capable of 845 kilometers (525 miles) on a full charge. By comparison, the iX in xDrive45 M Sport specification can't do better than 602 kilometers (374 miles) in one go. Naturally, the iX5 also fares better in terms of charging, with BMW claiming up to 406 instead of 175 kilowatts.\nThat's 2.6 times the peak rate, and yes, prospective customers need a charger that is capable of supplying that kind of power to actually experience charging at 460 kilowatts. From 10 to 80 percent state of charge, the iX5 needs only 23 minutes.\nA quick search on BMW's media website also reveals that 16,789 examples of the iX were delivered globally in the first nine months of 2026, representing a 34.1 percent nosedive from the 25,463 moved a year earlier. The plug-in hybrid XM performance SUV also fell badly, with BMW reporting 4,115 versus 5,732 deliveries.\nVia // CarExpert\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBMW iX BMW iX5 BMW EV Australia\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Ridden: Ausom's Dual-Motor GoSoul 2 Surprises With Speed, Comfort, and Acceleration for Low Bucks",
